       A  process  has a log priority mask that determines which calls to ssyyss‐‐
       lloogg(3) may be logged.  All other calls will be ignored.  Logging is en‐
       abled for the priorities that have the corresponding bit set  in  _m_a_s_k.
       The initial mask is such that logging is enabled for all priorities.

       The  sseettllooggmmaasskk()  function  sets this logmask for the calling process,
       and returns the previous mask.  If the _m_a_s_k argument is 00, the  current
       logmask is not modified.

       The  eight  priorities  are  LLOOGG__EEMMEERRGG,  LLOOGG__AALLEERRTT,  LLOOGG__CCRRIITT, LLOOGG__EERRRR,
       LLOOGG__WWAARRNNIINNGG, LLOOGG__NNOOTTIICCEE, LLOOGG__IINNFFOO, and LLOOGG__DDEEBBUUGG.  The bit  correspond‐
       ing  to a priority _p is _L_O_G___M_A_S_K_(_p_).  Some systems also provide a macro
       _L_O_G___U_P_T_O_(_p_) for the mask of all priorities in the above list up to  and
       including _p.

